## 6. Frame Timing & Interpolation

### RequestAnimationFrame with Delta-Time

Fixed frame advancement leads to inconsistent animation speed across different display refresh rates (60Hz, 120Hz, 144Hz). Always use **delta-time normalisation**:

```javascript
let lastTime = 0;
const FRAME_DURATION = 100;  // ms per animation frame at 60fps

function gameLoop(timestamp) {
  const deltaTime = timestamp - lastTime;
  lastTime = timestamp;

  // Accumulate time and advance animation frames based on real elapsed time
  animator.frameAccumulator += deltaTime;
  while (animator.frameAccumulator >= FRAME_DURATION) {
    animator.advanceFrame();
    animator.frameAccumulator -= FRAME_DURATION;
  }

  // Frame interpolation for super-smooth rendering
  const interpolationFactor = animator.frameAccumulator / FRAME_DURATION;
  animator.drawInterpolated(ctx, interpolationFactor);  // blends between current and next frame

  requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
}
requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
```

### Frame Interpolation (Between Two Adjacent Sprite Frames)

Interpolating between consecutive sprite frames via alpha blending produces **smoother animation than simply cycling frames**, especially on 120Hz+ displays:

```javascript
drawInterpolated(ctx, factor) {
  const currentFrame = this.frameCache.get(`${this.state}_${this.currentFrame}`);
  const nextFrame = this.frameCache.get(`${this.state}_${this.nextFrame}`);

  ctx.globalAlpha = 1 - factor;
  ctx.drawImage(currentFrame, this.x, this.y);

  ctx.globalAlpha = factor;
  ctx.drawImage(nextFrame, this.x, this.y);

  ctx.globalAlpha = 1;  // reset
}
```

